 ],
 "literal": "Having become a shade to many, the world praising highly, / because of not reaching the natural end of life, shrinking into a narrow path, / even when having become a permanent hero stone, / having given space, they will surely bestow grace, / with the body, the sweet life desiring, the bond of / those who have ancient friendship, if they go near them.",
 "translation": "He who was a protective shade to many, highly praised by the world, did not live out his full span of life but shrank into a narrow path. Even though he has now become a permanent hero stone, he will surely make room and welcome me, if those with ancient friendship—a bond as close as that between the body and the sweet life that loves it—go near him.",
 "modern": "பலருக்குப் புகலிடமாக விளங்கி, உலகத்தாரால் பெரிதும் புகழப்பட்டு, முழுமையான ஆயுளை அடையாமல் வடக்கிருந்து உயிர் துறந்து, நிலைபெற்ற நடுகல்லாக மாறிய பின்பும், உடம்போடு கூடிய இனிய உயிரைப் போன்ற நெருக்கமான பழைய நட்பினை உடையவர்கள் அவரிடம் சென்றால், அவர் நிச்சயம் இடம் கொடுத்து அருள் செய்வார்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the poet Pōttiyār, a close friend of the Chola king Kōpperuñcōḻaṉ. When the king decided to fast unto death (vaṭakkiruttal), Pōttiyār wanted to die with him, but the king asked him to wait until his child was born. After the birth of his child, Pōttiyār returned to the spot where the king was commemorated with a hero stone (naṭukal). The poet expresses his deep grief and absolute faith in their friendship, saying that even though the king is now just a stone, he will still make room for his friend. The poem beautifully compares ancient friendship (toṉṉaṭpu) to the inseparable bond between the body (uṭampu) and the sweet life (iṉṉuyir) that dwells within it. It highlights the immortality of true friendship, which transcends death itself.",
